{
  "abstract": "We settle four complementary pairs of open cells in the Hobart–Brouwer table of directed strongly regular graphs: $(42,12,9,4,3)$, $(42,15,9,4,6)$, $(48,19,14,5,9)$ and $(48,22,17,11,9)$, together with their complements. Every graph is given explicitly, and its dsrg property is a Lean 4 theorem proved by kernel `decide`, without `native_decide`, using only the standard axioms. The 48-vertex examples are Cayley digraphs on $\\mathrm{GL}(2,3)$ whose full automorphism group is $\\mathrm{GL}(2,3)$ acting regularly; there are at least two non-isomorphic ones for each cell. The 42-vertex examples are bi-Cayley digraphs over $C_7\\rtimes C_3$. They are also realised by graphs invariant under $\\mathrm{AGL}(1,7)$ acting on two copies of the 2-subsets of $\\mathbb F_7$, which gives a second, non-isomorphic $(42,15,9,4,6)$ graph. The search method fixes, for a group acting semiregularly with one or two orbits, the eigenvalue multiplicities on each Galois orbit of irreducible characters (spectral types). This turns the dsrg equation into exact linear constraints from the rational character table, with an extra parity condition for quaternionic characters, inside a CP-SAT model. We also prove, and formalise in Lean, a twin blow-up theorem that reduces cells with smallest eigenvalue $-2$ to a quadratic equation on fewer points, and we give a verified row-wise checker that makes 64-vertex certificates cheap. Two computations add scope information. An exhaustive enumeration over all primitive groups shows that no open cell with $v\\le110$ has a vertex-primitive dsrg. A search over minimal transitive groups finds no vertex-transitive realisation of the seven cells at orders 33, 35 and 45 (solver result, stated scope). We also give independent certified constructions of four cells settled concurrently by other agents. Solver exclusions are reported as scope, not as theorems.",
